course: dictionary
lesson: 872
title: A billion, and billions
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A billion is one thousand million, written 1,000,000,000. That is a one followed by nine zeros. Each comma marks a group of three zeros, so there are three groups of three zeros after the one.
explain: The word «billions» is used in a looser way. «The Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy» means there are so many stars that you could never count them one by one. «Billions of» means very many.
explain: Context helps you check the size. «Your brain has about 86 billion nerve cells» tells you the number is huge. Say it as eighty-six billion, and you can write it as 86,000,000,000.
fact: A billion is one thousand million: 1,000,000,000.
fact: Each comma in 1,000,000,000 marks a group of three zeros.
fact: In science, the Earth is about 4.5 billion years old, and there are about 8 billion people.
fact: «Billions of» means a very large number of things, too many to count.
fact: A computer chip can hold billions of tiny switches, each one smaller than a grain of sand.
exercise: «The Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy.» What does billions of mean here? | Fewer than a hundred | Only ten | Exactly two | *Very many
exercise: How many zeros are there in one billion? | Three | Six | Twelve | *Nine
exercise: Which number is one billion? | 1,000 | *1,000,000,000 | 1,000,000 | 100,000,000,000
test: How many thousands make one billion? | 1,000 | 10,000,000 | *1,000,000 | 100,000
test: «Your brain has about 86 billion nerve cells.» How do you write 86 billion in digits? | *86,000,000,000 | 86,000 | 8,600,000,000,000 | 860,000,000
exam: Which number is one thousand million? | *1,000,000,000 | 10,000,000,000 | 100,000 | 1,000,000
